<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern Yan</h1><p class="st-meta">407 CE – 436 CE · capital Longcheng</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last of the north-eastern Yan states, ruled from Longcheng first by Gao Yun and then by the Chinese Feng family, until the Northern Wei conquest of 436.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 407 the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Gao Yun</a>, a man of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> descent adopted into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a> royal family, killed the tyrannical emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xi">Murong Xi</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaoyang-liaoning">Longcheng</a> (Chaoyang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaoning">Liaoning</a>). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao">Gao</a> Yun became Heavenly King of a new Yan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Gao Yun</a> was assassinated in 409, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-ba">Feng Ba</a> took the throne and ruled moderately, reducing taxes and encouraging farming. Northern Yan kept close ties with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a>. Feng Ba's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-hong">Feng Hong</a> seized power in 430 amid family bloodshed, and the state was soon besieged by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 436 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> armies closed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaoyang-liaoning">Longcheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-hong">Feng Hong</a> fled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/goguryeo">Goguryeo</a> with his people; he was killed there in 438. His granddaughter became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-feng">Empress Dowager Feng</a>, who later reformed the Northern Wei.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-yan.jpg" alt="Northern Yan" width="320" height="226"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sixteen_Kingdoms_423_AD_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>407 CE – 436 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Longcheng</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gao Yun, Feng Ba, Feng Hong</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yan (Feng clan)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=422&amp;focus=northern-yan" aria-label="Northern Yan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7384 988 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Northern Yan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8033 1602h0M7771 1192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7704 1244h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7704 1244h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=422&amp;focus=northern-yan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
